// std/relation.bpa — binary relations and equivalence relations over a fixed
// element domain, per Chapter 1 §1.2.3 of Judson's *Abstract Algebra*
// (vendor/aata/src/sets.xml).
//
// A relation on X is modeled as a binary predicate `related(x, y)` over a sort
// `Element` (the analogue of `member` for sets / `apply` for functions). Its
// structural properties — reflexive, symmetric, transitive, and being an
// equivalence relation — are predicates fixed by defining axioms (each an `and`
// of the two implications, since bpa has no `<->`), exactly like `injective` /
// `surjective` in std/function.bpa.
//
// This is the FOL-tractable layer of §1.2.3. What it does NOT model:
// equivalence CLASSES `[a]` and PARTITIONS (a set X = ⋃[x] of classes) — those
// need a quotient / sets-of-sets structure, deferred to a later theory. Every
// theorem here is about the relation itself, membershipwise-free.

sort Element
pred related(x: Element, y: Element)
pred reflexive
pred symmetric
pred transitive
pred equivalence

// reflexive: x ∼ x for every x.
axiom reflexiveDef:
  (reflexive -> (forall x: Element; related(x, x)))
  and ((forall x: Element; related(x, x)) -> reflexive)

// symmetric: x ∼ y implies y ∼ x.
axiom symmetricDef:
  (symmetric -> (forall x, y: Element; related(x, y) -> related(y, x)))
  and ((forall x, y: Element; related(x, y) -> related(y, x)) -> symmetric)

// transitive: x ∼ y and y ∼ z imply x ∼ z.
axiom transitiveDef:
  (transitive -> (forall x, y, z: Element; related(x, y) -> related(y, z) -> related(x, z)))
  and ((forall x, y, z: Element; related(x, y) -> related(y, z) -> related(x, z)) -> transitive)

// an equivalence relation is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.
axiom equivalenceDef:
  (equivalence -> (reflexive and symmetric and transitive))
  and ((reflexive and symmetric and transitive) -> equivalence)
